 "_evidence": "0x0053dca6 (menu navigation; compose after menu_0084300a+sel_0053d8c5+items_0053dc57, globals=layout only so the static next pointers, ids, +0xe value pointers and +0x1a handlers keep their image values): every caller passes ebx = 0x22/0x23/0x24/0x25 (0x53d799/0x53d7a4/0x53d7af/0x53d7ba and 0x53db52 'mov ebx,0x2N') and esi = the selected item of the current menu (0x53db52 hops [0x82fd45] times from [[0x82fd34]]), so esi is one of the 13 static items 0x843040..0x843224 of the menu 0x84300a. 'movzx eax,byte [ebx+edi]' reads the direction link of the item: 0 returns, 1..[0x82fd52]=13 hops 'mov edi,[edi]; loop' from the header (valid for 1..13), above the count or 0xfc..0xff goes to the empty stub 0x54e232, 0xfa/0xfb call [0x82feac+4*(link-0xfa)] = 0x53dd23/0x53dd3a only while edi == esi (they read/write the dword [[esi+0xe]] (+4/+8) = 0x81e4f8.. or 0x7ab544.., static data, then call 0x53dc57 whose domain items_0053dc57 provides). A target whose +0x1f has 0x10 or 0x20 (varied by menu_0084300a) is skipped by re-reading ITS link at the same offset, so a chain of disabled items that returns to an earlier item never terminates (the game's menus never form such a cycle: it would hang the menu). Each item's four link bytes (+0x22..+0x25, real item strides 0x26/0x2a) are therefore varied per direction over an acyclic graph: offsets 0x22/0x24 link item k only to items k+1..13, offsets 0x23/0x25 only to items 1..k-1 (both forward and backward links occur), plus 0 (twice), 0xfa, 0xfb, 0xfc, 0xff and 14/0xf9 (above the count). Every walk ends after at most 13 hops, so nothing hangs or faults, and disabled chains that end in 0, a special code at a non-entry item (0x53dce8 taken) or a rejected index all occur. The 0xfb handler 0x53dd3a also reads the scroll record behind +0xe ({pos, max +4, page +8}: 0x81e4f8/0x81e4fc/0x81e500 for items 1-5, 0x7ab544/0x7ab548/0x7ab54c for items 6-13; menu_0084300a varies pos), so max and page vary over small signed values (-2..40) and both clamps (pos+1+page > max, result < 0) occur; only add/sub/cmp read them, so no value faults.",
